1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,056
Results day.

2
00:00:01,056 --> 00:00:04,780
You type your symbol number into the board's website and press check.

3
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:06,460
Two kinds of program run.

4
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:11,382
In your browser, JavaScript checks that you typed a sensible number, instantly.

5
00:00:11,382 --> 00:00:18,065
Then the request travels to the server, where PHP looks you up in a database and builds a page just for you.

6
00:00:18,267 --> 00:00:21,745
Client side and server side, working together.

7
00:00:21,745 --> 00:00:28,141
This unit teaches both: JavaScript for the browser, and PHP with MySQL for the server.

8
00:00:33,633 --> 00:00:38,763
Welcome to Class 12 Computer Science, Unit 3: Web Technology Two.

9
00:00:41,133 --> 00:00:42,333
Seven parts.

10
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:45,015
Client side and server side scripting.

11
00:00:45,015 --> 00:00:46,890
JavaScript basics.

12
00:00:46,890 --> 00:00:49,468
Control structures and functions.

13
00:00:49,468 --> 00:00:52,958
Objects, events and form validation.

14
00:00:52,958 --> 00:00:54,715
PHP basics.

15
00:00:54,715 --> 00:00:57,489
PHP objects and operators.

16
00:00:57,489 --> 00:01:00,626
And connecting PHP to a MySQL database.

17
00:01:04,967 --> 00:01:09,252
A scripting language is interpreted line by line as it runs.

18
00:01:09,252 --> 00:01:12,039
On the web there are two places a script can run.

19
00:01:12,039 --> 00:01:18,680
Client side scripts, in JavaScript, are downloaded with the page and run in the visitor's browser.

20
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:26,774
Server side scripts, like PHP, run on the web server before the page is sent; the visitor only ever sees their output.

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:28,913
Compare them.

22
00:01:28,913 --> 00:01:39,460
Client side code is visible to anyone, answers instantly and costs the server nothing, but cannot reach the database and can be switched off by the user.

23
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:47,058
Server side code is hidden and secure, and can use the database, but every action needs a trip to the server.

24
00:01:48,267 --> 00:01:51,120
Type an address and five things happen.

25
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:57,266
DNS, the internet's directory, turns the domain name into an IP address.

26
00:01:57,266 --> 00:02:00,547
The browser sends an HTTP request.

27
00:02:00,547 --> 00:02:06,055
The server runs any script and sends back HTML, which the browser draws.

28
00:02:06,055 --> 00:02:11,901
A URL has a protocol, a domain, a path and sometimes a query string.

29
00:02:11,901 --> 00:02:15,234
HTTPS is the same protocol, encrypted.

30
00:02:20,033 --> 00:02:25,009
JavaScript lives in script tags in the page, or in a separate file.

31
00:02:25,009 --> 00:02:31,831
Variables are declared with var, let or const, and need no type: the value decides it.

32
00:02:31,831 --> 00:02:38,381
Number, string, boolean, null, undefined, and objects such as arrays.

33
00:02:38,381 --> 00:02:40,384
The typeof operator tells you which.

34
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:42,682
Operators.

35
00:02:42,682 --> 00:02:46,133
Arithmetic, including percent for the remainder.

36
00:02:46,133 --> 00:02:55,990
Then a favourite exam question: seventeen double equals the string seventeen is true, because JavaScript converts the string to a number first.

37
00:02:55,990 --> 00:02:59,115
Triple equals is false: the types differ.

38
00:02:59,115 --> 00:03:02,734
Use triple equals unless you really want the conversion.

39
00:03:02,734 --> 00:03:04,794
And plus between strings joins them.

40
00:03:09,567 --> 00:03:14,087
Control structures decide which statements run and how often.

41
00:03:14,087 --> 00:03:17,420
A for loop has a start, a test and a step.

42
00:03:17,420 --> 00:03:26,691
Trace it: i is one, sum becomes one; i is two, sum becomes three; and so on, until i passes ten.

43
00:03:26,691 --> 00:03:28,220
The sum is fifty five.

44
00:03:29,433 --> 00:03:40,125
The full set: if else for choices, switch for one of many values, for for a known count, while which tests first, and do while which always runs at least once.

45
00:03:40,125 --> 00:03:46,883
A function is a named block that takes parameters and can return a value: area of eight by five is forty.

46
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:48,688
A classic.

47
00:03:49,333 --> 00:03:52,228
Loop i from one to ten and write one line each time.

48
00:03:52,433 --> 00:03:56,016
The output: seven, fourteen, up to seventy.

49
00:03:56,233 --> 00:04:03,305
Without the brackets, plus works left to right on a string, so seven and one are glued on: seventy one.

50
00:04:03,305 --> 00:04:06,228
The brackets force the multiplication first.

51
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:17,075
An object bundles data, its properties, with functions, its methods.

52
00:04:17,075 --> 00:04:24,302
A constructor function builds objects: new Student creates one with a name, marks, and a result method.

53
00:04:24,302 --> 00:04:30,322
JavaScript is called object based: it has objects and built in ones like Math and Array.

54
00:04:31,533 --> 00:04:37,706
An event is something that happens on the page: a click, a key press, the page loading.

55
00:04:37,706 --> 00:04:41,456
An event handler is the function that runs in response.

56
00:04:41,456 --> 00:04:48,541
Click the button: onclick calls greet, which finds the paragraph by its id and changes its contents.

57
00:04:49,767 --> 00:04:53,714
Form validation checks what the user typed before it is sent.

58
00:04:53,714 --> 00:05:00,810
The form's onsubmit calls a function; returning false cancels the submission, returning true lets it go.

59
00:05:00,810 --> 00:05:04,664
The browser check is for convenience: instant feedback.

60
00:05:04,664 --> 00:05:09,839
But users can switch JavaScript off, so the server must check again before anything is saved.

61
00:05:11,067 --> 00:05:16,850
jQuery is a JavaScript library whose motto is write less, do more.

62
00:05:16,850 --> 00:05:26,894
It wraps everything in one function, the dollar sign: select by id, change content, handle clicks, and animate, each in a short line.

63
00:05:31,667 --> 00:05:44,807
PHP, created by Rasmus Lerdorf in nineteen ninety four, runs on a web server stack: an operating system, the Apache web server, the PHP interpreter and a MySQL database.

64
00:05:44,807 --> 00:05:46,148
XAMPP bundles them.

65
00:05:46,148 --> 00:05:52,710
Save your file in the htdocs folder, start Apache and MySQL, and open it through localhost.

66
00:05:52,710 --> 00:05:55,990
Double clicking the file will not run the PHP at all.

67
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:03,217
PHP code sits between less than question mark php and question mark greater than.

68
00:06:03,217 --> 00:06:08,725
Every variable starts with a dollar sign, and every statement ends with a semicolon.

69
00:06:08,725 --> 00:06:13,530
An associative array stores key value pairs, and foreach walks through them.

70
00:06:13,530 --> 00:06:16,839
Double quotes substitute variables into the text.

71
00:06:21,633 --> 00:06:25,359
Unlike JavaScript, PHP has full classes.

72
00:06:25,359 --> 00:06:29,968
A class declares properties and methods; the constructor sets them up.

73
00:06:29,968 --> 00:06:34,538
Inside the class, this arrow refers to the object's own members.

74
00:06:34,538 --> 00:06:39,903
new creates an object, and the arrow, not a dot, reaches its members.

75
00:06:39,903 --> 00:06:41,669
extends gives inheritance.

76
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:45,154
A trap when switching languages.

77
00:06:45,154 --> 00:06:51,482
In PHP, plus always adds: the string five plus three is the number eight.

78
00:06:51,482 --> 00:06:54,868
To join strings, PHP uses a dot.

79
00:06:54,868 --> 00:06:59,126
JavaScript does the opposite: plus with a string joins.

80
00:06:59,126 --> 00:07:06,128
And remember precedence: multiplication before addition, so two plus three times four is fourteen.

81
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:16,188
Every PHP program that uses a database follows the same five steps.

82
00:07:16,188 --> 00:07:20,876
Connect, with the host, user, password and database name.

83
00:07:20,876 --> 00:07:22,204
Check the connection.

84
00:07:22,204 --> 00:07:23,271
Send a query.

85
00:07:23,271 --> 00:07:29,521
Fetch the rows, usually in a while loop, because fetch returns false when the rows run out.

86
00:07:29,521 --> 00:07:30,733
And close the connection.

87
00:07:31,933 --> 00:07:37,416
The commonest practical question: print a query's rows as an HTML table.

88
00:07:37,416 --> 00:07:43,432
Echo the opening table tag, then loop: each fetched row becomes one table row.

89
00:07:43,432 --> 00:07:45,307
The browser draws the result.

90
00:07:45,307 --> 00:07:52,090
fetch assoc gives an array keyed by column name; fetch row by number; fetch object gives an object.

91
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:56,778
Never paste user input straight into a query.

92
00:07:56,778 --> 00:08:04,291
If someone types quote, OR one equals one, the condition becomes true for every row, and they get everything.

93
00:08:04,291 --> 00:08:05,958
That is SQL injection.

94
00:08:05,958 --> 00:08:15,383
Use a prepared statement instead: the query has a question mark placeholder, and the value is bound separately, so it can never change the query's meaning.

95
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:18,570
Pause and think.

96
00:08:25,867 --> 00:08:30,921
False: triple equals checks the type too, and a number is not a string.

97
00:08:30,921 --> 00:08:32,373
Double equals would say true.

98
00:08:34,367 --> 00:08:34,802
One more.

99
00:08:42,433 --> 00:08:45,690
It can access the database: that is its main job.

100
00:08:45,690 --> 00:08:47,678
Its source never reaches the browser.

101
00:08:49,867 --> 00:08:50,962
Quick recap.

102
00:08:50,962 --> 00:08:54,725
JavaScript in the browser, PHP on the server.

103
00:08:54,725 --> 00:08:58,658
JavaScript types, operators and control structures.

104
00:08:58,658 --> 00:09:00,624
Events and validation.

105
00:09:00,624 --> 00:09:03,371
PHP syntax and classes.

106
00:09:03,371 --> 00:09:09,181
And the five steps of database connectivity, with prepared statements against SQL injection.

107
00:09:13,367 --> 00:09:15,074
That was web technology.

108
00:09:15,074 --> 00:09:21,051
Next, programming in C: functions, pointers, structures and files.

109
00:09:21,051 --> 00:09:21,592
See you there.

